<TopicPage slug="subjects/sociology/intermediate/race-ethnicity-and-identity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Race and ethnicity are <strong>society's systems of categorising people — with real
    consequences despite unreal biology</strong>. The sociology tracks how categories are made,
    imposed, resisted and remade, and how inequality reproduces through mechanisms that no longer
    require anyone to intend it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="construction that works, the key distinction, mechanisms without malice, models of integration" widget="equationStep" config={raceCfg} />

  <h2>Concepts that organise the evidence</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Whiteness expanded historically:</strong> Irish, Italian and Jewish Americans were not "white" on arrival (Ignatiev, Brodkin) — categories move as power moves. Race is a verb as much as a noun.</li>
    <li><strong>Caste vs race:</strong> Wilkerson's framework argues caste (fixed hierarchy) describes the American system better than race (category); critics reply the lens overgeneralises India. The debate itself models careful comparison (the anthropology lesson's method).</li>
    <li><strong>Measured consequences:</strong> wealth gaps (not income — wealth compounds; the finance lesson's mathematics applied unequally), health disparities (weathering — stress measured in cells), and neighbourhood effects (residential segregation concentrates poverty's effects).</li>
    <li><strong>Identity as project:</strong> from Du Bois's "double consciousness" to modern identity politics — the studied insistence on self-definition is itself sociological data, not noise around it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Du Bois, The Souls of Black Folk (1903); Bertrand & Mullainathan (2004); Omi & Winant, Racial Formation (1986); Wilkerson, Caste (2020).</p>
</TopicPage>
